**Rules for an immutable class in Java:**
1. Declare the class `final` (or make all constructors private) so no subclass can add mutability.
2. Make all fields `private final`.
3. No setters or any method that modifies state.
4. Initialise all fields in the constructor.
5. For fields holding **mutable objects**, make **defensive copies** in the constructor and in getters; never expose or store the caller's reference.
6. Do not let `this` escape from the constructor.

**Working example:**

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Date;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Objects;

public final class Employee {
    private final int id;
    private final String name;
    private final Date joined;           // mutable type
    private final List<String> skills;   // mutable type

    public Employee(int id, String name, Date joined, List<String> skills) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = Objects.requireNonNull(name);
        this.joined = new Date(joined.getTime());                     // defensive copy
        this.skills = Collections.unmodifiableList(new ArrayList<>(skills));
    }

    public int getId() { return id; }
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public Date getJoined() { return new Date(joined.getTime()); }    // copy out
    public List<String> getSkills() { return skills; }                // already unmodifiable

    public Employee withName(String newName) {                        // "modify" by returning a new object
        return new Employee(id, newName, joined, skills);
    }

    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof Employee)) return false;
        Employee e = (Employee) o;
        return id == e.id && name.equals(e.name)
            && joined.equals(e.joined) && skills.equals(e.skills);
    }
    @Override public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(id, name, joined, skills); }
}
```

**Why it works:** `String` and wrapper types are already immutable; `Date` and `List` are not, so we copy in and out. A shallow `unmodifiableList` of a copied list is safe because the elements (`String`) are immutable.

**Benefits:** inherently thread-safe with no synchronization, safe as `HashMap` keys, easy to reason about and cache. **Costs:** a new object for each change. In modern Java, mention `record` (Java 16+), which gives immutable data carriers (still copy mutable components) and `java.time` types instead of `Date`.
